pkg/proc: let the detsched runtime count a hit-count breakpoint's hits

A breakpoint costs a trap on every hit, about a millisecond, even when
its hit condition is false, so "cond -hitcount bp == 1000000" on a hot
function took a thousand seconds to reach its hit.

In a detsched program, when a breakpoint sits at a function's entry,
before its entry tick, and its only condition is on its total hit count
(== N, >= N, > N or % N), the debugger leaves it out of the program and
has the runtime count the function's entries instead: it makes the
entry tick's conditional branch always call runtime.detPreempt, which
counts the calls whose return address is in runtime.detDebug.watch and
calls runtime.detDebugWatch at the entry before the one the condition
asks for. An internal breakpoint there writes the user's breakpoint,
which the next entry hits with the hit count stock Delve would have
counted. The entries before it cost a call instead of a trap. The
schedule is unchanged: the patched tick calls detPreempt, which returns
at once without counting a tick.

breakpoints shows such a breakpoint as "hits counted by the runtime".

This is specific to detsched: it needs the runtime's side, in
src/runtime/detstop.go.

